body:
> The proposed fix appears to fix the crash. However, if I add a type annotation `cls: Type['Foo']`, there's a warning
> 
> ```
> test.py:12: error: The erased type of self "Type[test.Foo]" is not a supertype of its class "test.Foo"
> ```
> 
> This warning does not appear prior to the proposed fix.

this issue is not related to the plugin, this involves the stubs setup for the declared_attr class.   same message if you turn off the plugin and use a simple example:

```py
from typing import Any
from typing import Type
from sqlalchemy.orm import declared_attr


class Foo:
    @declared_attr
    def anything(cls: Type[Foo]) -> dict[Any, Any]:
        return {}
```

mypy will accept the class if you put classmethod on top and this seems to not interfere with declarative's work:


```py
from typing import Any
from typing import Type
from sqlalchemy.orm import declared_attr


class Foo:
    @classmethod
    @declared_attr
    def anything(cls: Type[Foo]) -> dict[Any, Any]:
        return {}
```

I dont know the correct way to make the declared_attr mimic the behavior of `@classmethod`, @CaselIT might have some ideas, but this would be on the stubs side. we can maybe look at the previous sqlalchemy-stubs package to see what they're doing.   I would also hope that there's a way to do this, i.e. that mypy isn't just hardcoding a rule for `@classmethod` specifically, but it wouldnt be the first time as they do hardcode some typing logic for things like `tuple`, where we on the outside cannot yet create tuple-like types of our own.

For the first example, I see the supertype error; however, if I change `Foo` to be derived from `declarative_base()` the error goes away.
```py
from typing import Any
from typing import Type
from sqlalchemy.orm import declared_attr
from sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base


Base = declarative_base()


class Foo(Base):
    @declared_attr
    def anything(cls: Type[Foo]) -> dict[Any, Any]:
        return {}
```

Same thing for if `__foo__` is used in place of `anything`; however the error returns with the proposed fix in place.
